# KeenGamer feed poll for Leonida Log (ADR 0007, 2026-11-27 note): runs /usr/local/sbin/leonida-log-scrape with
# --feed-only, which starts the scraper container from the deployed image and reads one URL, keengamer.com/feed/.
# leonida-log-feed.timer starts it every 4 hours. The feed holds only the newest 20 items (about 9 hours), so a
# poll that never runs loses articles; the weekly scrape reads the feed too but can't make that up.
#
# Same script as the weekly scrape, so it takes the same deploy lock, but only for the container's life (well
# under a minute; a stuck request ends after at most a few minutes). A deploy that starts meanwhile waits for it.
# A poll that starts during a deploy or the weekly scrape waits at most 60 s for the lock, then exits 75 without
# polling: it gives way and the next poll picks the items up. SuccessExitStatus=75 keeps that from counting as a
# failed unit.
#
# The run ends by itself: --feed-only gives the scraper a hard 180 s deadline (scrapers/main.ts, FEED_DEADLINE_MS)
# that aborts every request and wait, robots.txt and retries included, and exits 2, with a retry budget of 2.
# TimeoutStartSec is a backstop above the worst case: 60 s lock wait + at most 60 s for docker compose to start
# the container + 180 s deadline = 300 s, and 7 min is 420 s. systemd therefore never kills a poll that holds the
# lock.
#
# No OnFailure=: one failed poll (a 5xx, a timeout) is routine and would page up to six times a day. Instead
# ExecStopPost runs /usr/local/sbin/leonida-log-feed-result after every poll, which counts consecutive failures and
# starts one host-alert@ alert on the second (two polls in a row is where articles start to drop out of the feed),
# then stays quiet until a successful poll resets the count. An exit 75 (the lock-yield above) is neither: the poll
# didn't run, so the count is left as it is. The leading `-` keeps a missing script from failing the unit, and the
# script itself always exits 0, so the unit's own result is never hidden. A feed that stays unreadable also fails the
# weekly scrape's keengamer listing (exit 2), which alerts too. See failed polls with
# `systemctl status leonida-log-feed` and `journalctl -u leonida-log-feed`.
# Installed as it is by deploy/install-host.sh; nothing in it is per-host.
[Unit]
Description=Leonida Log KeenGamer feed poll
Requires=docker.service
After=docker.service network-online.target
Wants=network-online.target

[Service]
Type=oneshot
ExecStart=/usr/local/sbin/leonida-log-scrape --feed-only
ExecStopPost=-/usr/local/sbin/leonida-log-feed-result
TimeoutStartSec=7min
SuccessExitStatus=75
Nice=10
